(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-neck-masses-neoplasms-quiz"]={"title":"Neck Masses and Neoplasms Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the standard of care investigation for a persistent neck mass?","choices":["Fine needle aspiration biopsy","Excisional biopsy","Positron emission tomography","Plain radiography of the neck"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspiration separates neoplasm from inflammation and carcinoma from lymphoma, and provides material for cytology, Gram stain and culture. A minimum of four separate needle passes is required for adequacy, because a single pass can miss a focal deposit within an otherwise reactive node. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which conditions are listed as non-infectious inflammatory causes of neck masses?","choices":["Tuberculosis, actinomycosis and cat scratch disease","Lymphoma, thyroid carcinoma and metastatic squamous cell carcinoma","Branchial cleft cyst, thyroglossal duct cyst and dermoid cyst","Sjogren syndrome, sarcoidosis, IgG4-related sialadenitis and Kawasaki disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These are generally autoimmune in origin, and most patients have other symptoms that point toward the underlying disorder. Recognising them matters because the workup and treatment differ entirely from an infective or neoplastic mass. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does the assessment of a suspicious neck nodule emphasise about specialist referral?","choices":["Refer only if the patient develops systemic symptoms","Referral is unnecessary if fine needle aspiration is benign","Refer early, especially if it persists after treatment","Refer only after three failed antibiotic courses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A trial of antibiotics is common practice, and the point at which that trial fails is exactly the point at which referral should happen rather than another course being prescribed. Early and aggressive treatment is needed in some neoplastic conditions, so delay costs more here than the referral does. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 19"},{"q":"What histological finding establishes the diagnosis of a thymic cyst?","choices":["Ectopic thyroid follicles","Filamentous branching organisms","Hassall corpuscles","Keratin debris within a squamous-lined sac"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hassall corpuscles are concentric epithelial structures found in the thymic medulla, so their presence identifies the tissue definitively. Imaging narrows the differential but cannot make the diagnosis, which is why biopsy is specified and treatment is surgical excision. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is the commonest cause of cervical adenopathy in patients with HIV?","choices":["Kaposi sarcoma","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Lymphoma","Idiopathic follicular hyperplasia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cervical adenopathy is present in 12 to 45 per cent of patients with HIV, and follicular hyperplasia accounts for most of it. It is a diagnosis of exclusion, though: tuberculosis, Pneumocystis, lymphoma and Kaposi sarcoma all have to be ruled out first, and treatment is treating the HIV. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which infection produces a unilateral neck mass with brawny reddish-brown overlying skin in a child?","choices":["Tuberculous adenitis","Actinomycosis","Cat scratch disease","Atypical mycobacterial infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-tuberculous mycobacterial adenitis is a paediatric disease presenting as a unilateral mass in the anterior triangle or parotid region, with characteristically brawny, reddish-brown skin, induration and pain, in a child who is otherwise well. Diagnosis is by acid-fast stain, culture and skin testing, and treatment is usually surgical excision. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 34"},{"q":"What imaging feature suggests a teratoma?","choices":["A rim-enhancing hypodense collection","Absence of any soft tissue component","Calcifications","Transillumination on ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Teratomas originate from pluripotent cells and therefore contain a mixture of tissue types, including elements that calcify. Computed tomography and magnetic resonance imaging show those calcifications, and they help distinguish a firm neonatal neck mass from the soft lymphangioma or the vascular haemangioma. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 28"},{"q":"Why does every excised thyroglossal duct cyst go for histopathology?","choices":["To rule out thyroid carcinoma","To confirm the diagnosis, which is otherwise impossible","To identify the infecting organism","To determine whether the hyoid was completely removed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The tract follows the thyroid's embryological descent, so it can contain ectopic thyroid tissue, and that tissue can harbour carcinoma. Because the cyst is otherwise a benign congenital lesion, the malignancy would go undetected unless every specimen is examined. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which structure crosses the muscular floor of the occipital triangle to pass deep to trapezius?","choices":["The phrenic nerve","The spinal accessory nerve","The vagus nerve","The hypoglossal nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The spinal accessory nerve runs across the occipital triangle's muscular floor before passing deep to trapezius, which leaves it superficial and exposed in the posterior triangle. That is why it is the classic nerve injured during posterior triangle surgery or lymph node biopsy, producing a painful drooping shoulder. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which deep cervical node is specifically concerned with drainage of the palatine tonsil?","choices":["The jugulodigastric node","The juguloomohyoid node","The submental node","The retroauricular node"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two deep cervical nodes carry named territories. The jugulodigastric node drains the palatine tonsil, which is why tonsillitis produces a tender node high in the jugular chain just below the angle of the jaw, and why that is the node examined in a sore throat. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 12"},{"q":"Primary thyroid lymphoma is associated with which underlying condition?","choices":["Prior head and neck irradiation","Hashimoto thyroiditis","Graves disease","Multiple endocrine neoplasia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most primary thyroid lymphomas are non-Hodgkin B cell tumours arising in a gland already infiltrated by lymphocytes from Hashimoto thyroiditis. That shared lymphoid population is exactly why fine needle aspiration alone cannot separate the two, and why an open biopsy with lymphoma staging is needed. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which muscle divides the neck into anterior and posterior triangles?","choices":["Trapezius","Omohyoid","Digastric","Sternocleidomastoid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The neck is bounded by the mandible above and the clavicle below, and the sternocleidomastoid runs obliquely across it, splitting it into an anterior and a posterior triangle. Those two are then subdivided further by the omohyoid and digastric muscles. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which of these appears on the list of MALIGNANT primary neck tumours?","choices":["Arteriovenous malformation","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour","Schwannoma","Carotid body paraganglioma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The malignant list comprises the sarcomas, malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumours, lymphoma, and metastasis from mucosal head and neck cancer, salivary malignancy or skin malignancy. The benign list is the vascular neoplasms, arteriovenous malformations, benign nerve tumours and lipomas. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 38"},{"q":"What lies DEEP to the contents of the supraclavicular triangle?","choices":["The parotid gland","The cupola of the pleural cavity","The thyroid gland","The submandibular gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The supraclavicular triangle contains the terminal subclavian artery, the roots, trunks and divisions of the brachial plexus, thyrocervical trunk branches and tributaries of the external jugular vein. The dome of the pleura sits deep to all of them, which is why a penetrating wound there can cause a pneumothorax. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 5"},{"q":"An immobile midline neck mass that elevates with swallowing most likely arises from what?","choices":["A lymph node","A branchial cleft cyst","The submandibular gland","The thyroid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The thyroid is attached to the larynx and trachea by the pretracheal fascia, so it rises when the larynx rises during a swallow. That movement identifies the gland as the source and separates a thyroid mass from a node or a cyst that stays still. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which regimen treats tuberculous cervical adenitis?","choices":["Penicillin","Amphotericin B","RIPE: rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol","Streptomycin alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cervical tuberculosis, called scrofula, is treated with the standard antituberculous combination. The full list also includes rifabutin and rifapentine, but RIPE is the traditional four-drug initial regimen, and the diagnosis rests on tuberculin skin testing with acid-fast stain and culture. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which thyroid carcinoma has a prognosis measured in months?","choices":["Medullary carcinoma","Anaplastic carcinoma","Papillary carcinoma","Follicular carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anaplastic carcinoma represents about 1 per cent of thyroid cancers, occurs in elderly patients, and comprises small cell, giant cell and spindle cell types. It results in death within 6 to 36 months and is resistant to all treatment modalities, which is why the surgical option is isthmectomy rather than curative resection. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which nodes drain the scalp, auricle and middle ear?","choices":["Occipital, retroauricular and parotid nodes","Submandibular nodes","Retropharyngeal nodes","Supraclavicular nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Superficial cervical nodes are named for where they sit, and their territories follow position: the occipital, retroauricular and parotid groups take lymph from the scalp, the auricle and the middle ear. All of them then drain onward into the deep cervical chain. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 11"},{"q":"From which gland does a ranula arise?","choices":["The parotid gland","A minor salivary gland of the palate","The sublingual gland","The submandibular gland"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A ranula is a mucocele or retention cyst arising from the sublingual gland in the floor of the mouth. It is called plunging when it extends through the mylohyoid muscle into the neck, and treatment is excision of the sublingual gland itself, because removing only the cyst leaves the source. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which additional lymph node areas should be palpated when assessing a cervical mass?","choices":["No other areas, as cervical nodes drain only the head","Inguinal, axillary and supraclavicular","Only the contralateral cervical chain","Popliteal and antecubital only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Palpating other nodal basins distinguishes localised regional disease from generalised lymphadenopathy, which points toward a systemic cause such as lymphoma, mononucleosis or HIV. The thyroid, liver and spleen are examined for the same reason, and any vascular abnormality is auscultated. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 15"},{"q":"Once metastatic squamous carcinoma is confirmed in a cervical node, what usually finds the primary?","choices":["A complete office examination of all mucosal surfaces","Positron emission tomography as the first test","Repeating the fine needle aspiration","Empirical radiotherapy to the whole neck"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Examining every mucosal surface of the head and neck, along with the thyroid, the salivary glands and the skin, usually locates the primary in the office. That matters because the primary determines the extent of surgery and the radiotherapy field, and treating the neck alone leaves the source seeding. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which thyroid carcinoma is commonest and has the best prognosis?","choices":["Papillary carcinoma","Follicular carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma","Anaplastic carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Papillary carcinoma accounts for about 75 per cent of thyroid cancers, arises from thyroid epithelial cells, is commonest in young women and has the best outlook of the four types. It tends to spread to local cervical lymph nodes rather than distantly, which is part of why the prognosis is favourable. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which sign is pathognomonic for a thyroglossal duct cyst?","choices":["Fluctuance with overlying erythema","Vertical movement with swallowing or tongue protrusion","Transillumination","Enlargement with crying or straining"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The thyroid descends from the foramen caecum at the tongue base through the hyoid during development, so a persistent tract tethers the cyst to the hyoid. Protruding the tongue pulls the hyoid and therefore the cyst upward, which no other neck mass does. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 23"},{"q":"Above what size are normal hyperplastic lymph nodes rarely found?","choices":["10 centimetres","2 centimetres","5 centimetres","0.5 centimetres"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reactive hyperplastic nodes are rarely larger than 2 centimetres, so exceeding that adds weight to the concern raised by the other features. It sits alongside the 1.5 centimetre threshold on the malignancy list, and both are reasons to keep measuring rather than estimating. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does a positron emission tomography scan measure?","choices":["Metabolic uptake of tagged glucose, higher in cancer and inflammation","The blood supply of a lesion","The presence of calcification","The cell type of a tumour"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Radiolabelled glucose accumulates where metabolism is high, which includes malignancy but also inflammation and infection. That dual sensitivity is why it is useful for staging and for finding an occult primary, and why a positive result cannot by itself establish cancer. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the standard operation for a thyroglossal duct cyst?","choices":["The Sistrunk operation","Simple excision of the cyst alone","Marsupialisation","Sclerotherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cyst is excised along with a cuff of tissue INCLUDING the centre of the hyoid bone, because the tract runs through the hyoid on its way to the tongue base. Leaving the central hyoid behind leaves tract with it, which is why simple cyst excision recurs. Care is taken not to injure the hypoglossal nerves. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 23"},{"q":"In a patient with a thyroid nodule and a LOW thyroid-stimulating hormone, what comes before fine needle aspiration?","choices":["Immediate thyroidectomy","A course of antithyroid medication for six months","A radionuclide scan with technetium","Contrast computed tomography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A suppressed thyroid-stimulating hormone raises the possibility that the nodule is producing hormone autonomously. The scan distinguishes a hot, hyperfunctioning nodule, which carries a low malignant risk and needs no biopsy, from a cold or warm nodule, which does. Doing it first avoids an unnecessary procedure. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which imaging is preferred for a pulsatile neck mass?","choices":["Ultrasound alone","Plain radiography","Positron emission tomography","Computed tomography angiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulsatility indicates a vascular lesion, and angiography defines the blood supply and the relationship to the carotid before any intervention. Magnetic resonance imaging can substitute, but angiography is generally still preferred for this specific question. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the commonest metastatic lesion to the neck?","choices":["Adenocarcinoma","Melanoma","Lymphoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Malignant neck tumours are usually metastatic squamous cell carcinoma from the skin or the upper aerodigestive tract, rather than primary neck tumours. That is why finding a squamous deposit in a node triggers a systematic examination of every mucosal surface, the thyroid, the salivary glands and the skin. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 39"},{"q":"A rapidly growing neck mass WITHOUT other infectious symptoms suggests what?","choices":["A lipoma","A branchial cleft cyst","Lymphoma","Reactive viral lymphadenopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The rule pairs tempo with context: rapid growth is usually infectious when other symptoms accompany it, and lymphoma when they do not. The absence of fever, sore throat or a preceding upper respiratory infection is what redirects a fast-growing mass toward malignancy. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"}]};
